// WHERE DOES THE FALL-LINE CANYON ACTUALLY COME FROM? — plan 20, open question 9.
//
// `probe/house/envelope.loft` compared four PROFILES and concluded that the anchor
// should be the run's middle. Then it grew two more rows and refuted its own
// conclusion:
//
//   held at middle   cut 455  fill 390  earth moved 845
//   lower + shift    cut 429  fill 416  earth moved 845   <- what already ships
//
// The lower envelope followed by `A8`'s uniform shift by the mean moves EXACTLY as
// much earth as anchoring at the middle. So the anchor is not what halves the earth —
// the settle and the balance together already reach it, and open question 9 was
// comparing the settle against half of itself.
//
// ⚠ WHICH LEAVES THE MEASUREMENT UNEXPLAINED. `probe/house/span.loft` walks a road
// down a 6-per-hex ramp through the real gesture and gets **cut 120, proud 1** — a
// road pinned at the BOTTOM of the run with no lift in it at all, which is neither
// row above. Something between the rule and the gesture is losing the balance.
//
// So this stops arguing about envelopes and asks where the earth goes, by laying the
// SAME strip three ways on the SAME ground:
//
//   stamp only    the strokes' writes, no settle at all — what the lookahead costs
//   per stroke    `road_lay`, which settles the whole run on every stroke — SHIPS
//   settled once  the same writes, then ONE `slope_settle` over the finished run
//
// ⚠ THE PREDICTION, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN. If `settled once` comes back balanced
// (cut and fill within a few units of each other) while `per stroke` comes back
// pinned at the bottom, then the canyon is the balance being applied per stroke and
// truncated away — `spoil_place`'s `cut / n` with `n` the whole run — and not the
// envelope the open question blames. If BOTH come back pinned, the balance is not
// firing at all and the envelope is still in the frame.
//
// ── WHAT IT FOUND, AND THE FIRST READING OF IT WAS WRONG ────────────────────────
//
// `settled once` did come back balanced — and the legality column, added because a row
// that moves least by not finishing is not a candidate, said it had **192 road-to-road
// edges over the limit, worst step 6**. It moved less earth because it did LESS WORK.
// ⚠ `SLOPE_PASSES` is 12 and a correction travels one cell a pass, so no single call
// can settle a 26-cell run however right its rule is. Without that column the probe
// would have recommended the arm that leaves a road with a six-fold step in it.
//
// The fair comparison is `to convergence`, and with both arms legal (`slope_owed 0`):
//
//   6 per hex   per stroke      cut 1500  fill   8   moved 1508   -120 … +7
//               to convergence  cut  855  fill 143   moved  998    -90 … +37
//   3 per hex   per stroke      cut  777  fill   0   moved  777    -55 … -3
//               to convergence  cut  400  fill  39   moved  439    -39 … +13
//
// ⚠ SO THE CANYON IS THE REPETITION, NOT THE ENVELOPE. The same rule run to the end
// once moves a third less earth and produces the profile a road builder does — a
// cutting at the head and an embankment at the foot — where the per-stroke version
// produces a pure cutting. Every disc overwrites four strokes' worth of already
// balanced cells with the raw lookahead grade, and each shift is then diluted across
// the whole growing run.
//
// ⚠ AND THE TWO GOALS ARE COUPLED IN THE CURRENT SHAPE, which is the finding under the
// finding: the per-stroke call is ALSO the only reason the run ends up legal at all —
// twenty-six calls of twelve passes is what converges it. Repetition is standing in
// for passes, and paying for it with the balance.
//
// ── AND WHICH HALF OF THE REPETITION COSTS THE EARTH — the `+ one balance` arm ───
//
// The settle is NOT the half to change. Left exactly as it ships, per stroke, and
// followed by ONE balance over the whole run against the natural ground:
//
//   6 per hex, walked DOWN   per stroke 1508   -120 … +7    -> one balance  848   -63 … +64
//   6 per hex, walked UP     per stroke 1439   +117 … -8    -> one balance  845   +63 … -62
//   3 per hex, walked DOWN   per stroke  777    -55 … -3    -> one balance  341
//   1 per hex, either way    per stroke  127     -5 … -3    -> one balance   25
//
// ⚠ **845 AND 848 ARE THE OPTIMUM `envelope.loft` COMPUTED** — `held at middle` and
// `lower + shift` both move 845 at this gradient. So the run's SHAPE was already right
// all along: the per-stroke settle produces a correct limit-slope profile and simply
// leaves it 57 units too low. One shift recovers the whole of the gap, and no envelope,
// anchor or relaxation changes at all.
//
// ⚠ AND THE LAST ROW IS WORTH ITS OWN LINE: at 1 per hex the road CAN follow the ground
// inside its own limit and the settle never fires — yet the shipped path still moves
// 127 units, because a cell's grade comes from the last disc that covered it and that
// disc trails the author by `ROAD_HALF`. The road sinks a uniform five units into
// ground it could have followed exactly. The same balance takes that to 25.
//
// ⚠ AND THE BALANCE MUST BE TWO-SIDED, which the mirror is what caught. The first
// version of this arm read `if owed > 0`, fired on the descent and did NOTHING on the
// ascent — `shift 0`, every column unchanged, reading exactly like an arm with nothing
// to fix. That is this tree's one-sided guard for the third time, in the instrument
// rather than in the rule. `spoil_place` has the same shape and is right within its
// scope: it measures after the stamp, and the settle it measures can only lower. A
// RUN-level balance is measured against the natural ground, where both signs are
// reachable, so it must answer to both.
use hex_editor;
use hex_world;
const U   = 0.25;
const G   = 400;                 // a datum high enough that a deep cut cannot reach ρ
const EPS = 10;                  // the editor's own ε
const N   = 26;                  // cells walked, matching `span.loft`

// The fall line the road is walked down: `n` cells falling `k` a hex along `q`.
fn ramp_q(k: integer) -> World {
  w = world_new(U, 0, EPS, 4).wn_world;
  for q in (0 - 40)..41 {
    for r in (0 - 8)..9 { ground_set(w, q, r, G - k * q, SURFACE_MAT); }
  }
  w
}

// The ground along the walked line, sampled before anything is written to it.
fn natural(w: World) -> vector<integer> {
  out: vector<integer> = [];
  for s in 0..N { out += [ground_h(w, s, 0)]; }
  out
}

// ⚠ MEASURED DOWN THE CENTRE LINE, and that is fair here for one stated reason: the
// ground falls along `q` only, so every cell of the strip at a given `q` starts at the
// same height and the cross-section carries no information the centre line does not.
fn report(name: text, nat: vector<integer>, w: World) {
  cut = 0;
  fill = 0;
  worst_cut = 0;
  worst_fill = 0;
  prof = "";
  for j in 0..N {
    // ⚠ `run_h`, NOT `ground_h` — `A9`'s instrument lesson: a caved cell's ground is
    // the rock over it, so the ground reader answers about the hillside.
    rh = run_h(w, j, 0, ROAD_MAT);
    if rh < 0 { continue; }
    d = rh - (nat[j] ?? 0);
    if j % 5 == 0 { prof += "{d} "; }
    if d < 0 {
      cut = cut - d;
      if 0 - d > worst_cut { worst_cut = 0 - d; }
    } else {
      fill = fill + d;
      if d > worst_fill { worst_fill = d; }
    }
  }
  // ⚠ AND THE PROFILE IS ASSERTED LEGAL RATHER THAN ASSUMED, over the whole STRIP and
  // not only the centre line. Without this the rows are a comparison of how much earth
  // three rules move without anyone checking that any of them finished the job — and a
  // row that moves least by leaving the run steeper than its limit is not a candidate,
  // it is a bug. `A9` measured a 27-unit step in a road whose limit is 1 while every
  // count read 0, so this asks the question in the shape the violation lives in.
  bad = 0;
  worst_step = 0;
  for bj in 0..N {
    for br in (0 - hex_editor::ROAD_HALF)..(hex_editor::ROAD_HALF + 1) {
      hh = run_h(w, bj, br, ROAD_MAT);
      if hh < 0 { continue; }
      for dir in 0..6 {
        (nq, nr) = hex_grid::hex_neighbor(bj, br, dir);
        nh = run_h(w, nq, nr, ROAD_MAT);
        if nh < 0 { continue; }
        st = hh - nh;
        if st < 0 { st = 0 - st; }
        if st > 1 { bad = bad + 1; }
        if st > worst_step { worst_step = st; }
      }
    }
  }
  // ⚠ AND `slope_owed` BESIDE IT, because that is the tree's OWN answer to *did the
  // relaxation finish* — `A4` built it precisely so a stalled settle is a refusal
  // rather than a silent stop. Counting edges here and asking `slope_owed` there are
  // two instruments on one claim, and a row where they disagree is a row to distrust.
  owed = slope_owed(w, 0, 0);
  println("    {name}  cut {cut} (worst {worst_cut})  fill {fill} (worst {worst_fill})"
        + "  earth moved {cut + fill}   edges over the limit {bad}"
        + " (worst step {worst_step})  slope_owed {owed}");
  println("                  road minus natural, every 5th cell: {prof}");
}

// The strokes' writes and nothing else — the disc `road_lay` stamps, at the grade it
// reads one cell past the strip. ⚠ Later strokes overwrite earlier ones, so a cell's
// final grade comes from the LAST disc that covered it, which trails the author by
// `ROAD_HALF` — the lookahead a cell actually gets is `2 * ROAD_HALF + 1`, not one.
//
// ⚠ AND IT ACCUMULATES WHAT IT TOOK, which is the load-bearing claim of the fix rather
// than a convenience: a run-level balance needs the natural ground and the stamp has
// overwritten it, so the proposal is that `road_lay`'s own `snat` — sampled per disc
// BEFORE writing — telescopes. A later stroke re-reading a paved cell reads the road
// the earlier one left, so the per-stroke differences should sum to
// (natural − final) exactly, with no registry of original heights anywhere.
// `main` checks that sum against the profile the world actually ends up holding, so
// the claim is measured before anything is built on it.
fn stamp(w: World, took: &integer) {
  for i in 0..N {
    ahead = ground_h(w, i + hex_editor::ROAD_HALF + 1, 0);
    for aq in (0 - hex_editor::ROAD_HALF)..(hex_editor::ROAD_HALF + 1) {
      for ar in (0 - hex_editor::ROAD_HALF)..(hex_editor::ROAD_HALF + 1) {
        q0 = i + aq;
        if hex_grid::hex_distance(q0, ar, i, 0) > hex_editor::ROAD_HALF { continue; }
        took = took + (ground_h(w, q0, ar) - ahead);
        ground_set(w, q0, ar, ahead, ROAD_MAT);
      }
    }
  }
}

fn main() {
  rl_spoil = 0;
  println("A road may climb 1 a hex. Walked down a ramp it cannot follow, the same");
  println("strip laid three ways — and the question is where the balance goes.");
  // ⚠ AND WALKED UP AS WELL AS DOWN. This tree has written a one-sided guard twice —
  // `faced_between` and `stroke_over_limit` — and both read exactly like a guard until
  // they were asked from the other end. A negative `k` is a ramp that RISES away from
  // the author, so every row below is run against its own mirror.
  for k in [1, 3, 6, 0 - 1, 0 - 6] {
    println("");
    if k < 0 {
      println("  ramp {0 - k} per hex over {N} cells, RISING — the mirror");
    } else {
      println("  ramp {k} per hex over {N} cells — the ground falls {k * (N - 1)}");
    }

    // stamp only — no settle, so this is what the LOOKAHEAD alone costs
    wa = ramp_q(k);
    nat = natural(wa);
    took = 0;
    stamp(wa, took);
    report("stamp only  ", nat, wa);
    // ⚠ THE TELESCOPING CLAIM, CHECKED AGAINST THE WORLD IT CLAIMS TO DESCRIBE.
    // `took` is the sum of per-stroke differences a `road_lay`-side accumulator would
    // see; `held` is what the store actually ends up short of the natural ground over
    // the SAME cells. They must agree exactly, or the fix has no datum.
    held = 0;
    for tq in (0 - hex_editor::ROAD_HALF)..(N + hex_editor::ROAD_HALF) {
      for tr in (0 - hex_editor::ROAD_HALF)..(hex_editor::ROAD_HALF + 1) {
        th = run_h(wa, tq, tr, ROAD_MAT);
        if th < 0 { continue; }
        held = held + ((G - k * tq) - th);
      }
    }
    verdict = "AGREE";
    if took != held { verdict = "DISAGREE — the accumulator has no datum"; }
    println("                  telescoped {took} vs the store's {held} -> {verdict}");

    // per stroke — `road_lay`, settling the whole run on every stroke. SHIPS.
    wb = ramp_q(k);
    natb = natural(wb);
    cl = 0;
    rs = 0;
    // ⚠ A DEBT PER ARM AND PER RAMP. It is run state, so one shared across the arms
    // would shift a world by earth taken out of a different one.
    rl_spoil = 0;
    for i in 0..N {
      ahead = ground_h(wb, i + hex_editor::ROAD_HALF + 1, 0);
      road_lay(wb, i, 0, ahead, hex_editor::ROAD_HALF, (ahead as float) * U, cl, rs, rl_spoil);
    }
    report("per stroke  ", natb, wb);

    // settled once — the same writes, then one settle over the finished run
    wc = ramp_q(k);
    natc = natural(wc);
    tookc = 0;
    stamp(wc, tookc);
    cl2 = 0;
    rs2 = 0;
    slope_settle(wc, 0, 0, cl2, rs2);
    report("settled once", natc, wc);

    // ⚠ AND ONCE IS NOT ENOUGH TO FINISH, which the legality column is what said.
    // `SLOPE_PASSES` is 12 and a correction propagates one cell a pass, so a 26-cell
    // run cannot converge in one call however right its rule is. This arm keeps
    // calling until the world reports nothing owing — the same rule, run to the end,
    // which is the only version that can fairly be compared with `per stroke`.
    wd = ramp_q(k);
    natd = natural(wd);
    tookd = 0;
    stamp(wd, tookd);
    rounds = 0;
    for _ in 0..40 {
      if slope_owed(wd, 0, 0) == 0 { break; }
      cl3 = 0;
      rs3 = 0;
      slope_settle(wd, 0, 0, cl3, rs3);
      rounds = rounds + 1;
    }
    report("to convergence", natd, wd);
    println("                  ({rounds} calls to converge — one is what the gesture makes)");

    // ⚠ AND WHICH HALF OF THE REPETITION COSTS THE EARTH? Two mechanisms are folded
    // together above — the balance truncated away per stroke (`cut / n` with `n` the
    // whole run) and the disc re-lowering already-balanced cells. This arm separates
    // them: the per-stroke settle EXACTLY as it ships, and then `A8`'s balance applied
    // once at the end against the natural ground the road replaced.
    //
    // ⚠ A UNIFORM SHIFT CANNOT BREAK THE LIMIT, which is the property `spoil_place`
    // already rests on — so this arm's legality column is a check on the arithmetic
    // rather than on the idea.
    we = ramp_q(k);
    nate = natural(we);
    cle = 0;
    rse = 0;
    rl_spoil = 0;
    for i in 0..N {
      ahead = ground_h(we, i + hex_editor::ROAD_HALF + 1, 0);
      road_lay(we, i, 0, ahead, hex_editor::ROAD_HALF, (ahead as float) * U, cle, rse, rl_spoil);
    }
    owed_e = 0;
    cells = 0;
    for eq in (0 - hex_editor::ROAD_HALF)..(N + hex_editor::ROAD_HALF) {
      for er in (0 - hex_editor::ROAD_HALF)..(hex_editor::ROAD_HALF + 1) {
        eh = run_h(we, eq, er, ROAD_MAT);
        if eh < 0 { continue; }
        cells = cells + 1;
        owed_e = owed_e + ((G - k * eq) - eh);
      }
    }
    // ⚠ TWO-SIDED, AND THE FIRST VERSION WAS NOT — which is this tree's one-sided
    // guard written a third time, in the instrument this time rather than in the rule.
    // `if owed > 0` fires on a road walked DOWN, where the run is net cut, and does
    // nothing at all on one walked UP, where the run is net FILLED: the mirror came
    // back `shift 0` and unchanged, reading exactly like an arm with nothing to fix.
    // `spoil_place` has the same shape (`if cut <= 0 { return; }`) and it is right
    // WITHIN ITS SCOPE — it measures after the stamp, and the settle it measures can
    // only lower. A RUN-level balance is measured against the natural ground, where
    // both signs are reachable, so it has to answer to both.
    eshift = 0;
    if cells > 0 { eshift = (owed_e / cells) ?? 0; }
    if eshift != 0 {
      for eq2 in (0 - hex_editor::ROAD_HALF)..(N + hex_editor::ROAD_HALF) {
        for er2 in (0 - hex_editor::ROAD_HALF)..(hex_editor::ROAD_HALF + 1) {
          eh2 = run_h(we, eq2, er2, ROAD_MAT);
          if eh2 < 0 { continue; }
          run_set(we, eq2, er2, ROAD_MAT, eh2 + eshift);
        }
      }
    }
    report("+ one balance", nate, we);
    println("                  (the shipped settle, then ONE shift of {eshift} over "
          + "{cells} cells)");
  }
  println("");
  println("⚠ COMPARE `per stroke` WITH `to convergence`, and ONLY those two — they are");
  println("   the rows where `slope_owed` is 0. `settled once` is kept because it is");
  println("   the reading that was nearly believed: it moves the least earth of all");
  println("   and it leaves a road with a six-fold step in it.");
  println("⚠ The same rule run to the end once moves a third less earth and puts a");
  println("   cutting at the head and an embankment at the foot. The canyon is the");
  println("   repetition, and no choice of envelope can close it.");
  println("⚠ AND `+ one balance` IS THE ROW THAT MATTERS: the shipped settle untouched,");
  println("   per stroke, then ONE two-sided shift over the whole run. 848 walked down");
  println("   and 845 walked up, against `envelope.loft`'s optimum of 845 — so the");
  println("   profile was always right and only its DATUM was wrong.");
}
